WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.581 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A recipe, a ribbon and a walking track all hide fraction puzzles.

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:07.652 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Your first job isn't to grab the numbers.

00:00:07.772 --> 00:00:11.100 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
It's to discover what the story is really asking!

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:19.572 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we're learning to interpret everyday fraction problems, choose a suitable strategy, and explain a solution that makes sense.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.060 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll identify the whole and the unit, so you know what each fraction describes.

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:29.268 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll choose an operation and make a model before you calculate.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.717 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll explain your answer with its unit, then check it against the story.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.504 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First, choose one whole.

00:00:35.624 --> 00:00:38.340 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its parts must be equal in size.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.097 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The outline of the model shows the entire whole, not just the coloured share.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.814 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here, three equal parts are chosen out of four.

00:00:46.934 --> 00:00:50.528 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The top number, the numerator, counts chosen parts.

00:00:50.648 --> 00:00:55.263 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The bottom number, the denominator, tells how many equal parts make the whole.

00:00:55.867 --> 00:01:00.464 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A recipe uses half a cup of oats and a quarter of a cup of nuts.

00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:04.214 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
What operation fits the question: how much altogether?

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:10.067 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First identify the whole: one cup.

00:01:10.187 --> 00:01:14.267 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Altogether tells us to combine the amounts, so choose addition.

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:17.203 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Rename one half as two quarters.

00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:20.647 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two quarters plus one quarter makes three quarters.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.534 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Mia uses three quarters of a cup altogether.

00:01:24.654 --> 00:01:29.377 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
That is more than half a cup and less than one cup, so the answer makes sense.

00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:34.441 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A useful answer tells the amount and its unit: three quarters of a cup.

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:40.333 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Compare it with the ingredients to check that nothing has disappeared or been counted twice.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.190 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A ribbon is two metres long.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.701 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We cut off three quarters of a metre.

00:01:45.821 --> 00:01:48.969 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Will the remaining length be more or less than one metre?

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.606 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now a ribbon is two metres long.

00:01:54.726 --> 00:01:57.175 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We cut off three quarters of a metre.

00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:59.262 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How much ribbon remains?

00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:02.248 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We want what remains, so subtract.

00:02:02.368 --> 00:02:05.134 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two metres equals eight quarter-metres.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.298 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Eight quarters minus three quarters is five quarters.

00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:12.208 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Five quarters is one and one quarter.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.152 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One and one quarter metres remain.

00:02:15.272 --> 00:02:18.847 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
On the number line, move back three quarter-steps from two.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.227 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The three quarters refers to one metre, not three quarters of the two-metre ribbon.

00:02:24.347 --> 00:02:28.592 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Naming the unit clearly prevents a very different calculation.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:33.114 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For a comparison, Ava walks three quarters of a kilometre.

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:36.028 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Leo walks five eighths of a kilometre.

00:02:36.148 --> 00:02:38.714 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Who walks farther, and by how much?

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:41.733 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Rename three quarters as six eighths.

00:02:41.853 --> 00:02:46.191 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Ava walks farther because six eighths is greater than five eighths.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:49.317 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Subtract five eighths from six eighths.

00:02:49.437 --> 00:02:52.570 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Ava walks one eighth of a kilometre farther.

00:02:52.933 --> 00:02:55.044 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here is a two-step problem.

00:02:55.164 --> 00:02:57.136 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A jug holds one litre.

00:02:57.256 --> 00:03:00.642 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We pour out a quarter of a litre, then another half a litre.

00:03:00.762 --> 00:03:02.347 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How much remains?

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.648 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A quarter plus a half is three quarters of a litre poured out.

00:03:06.768 --> 00:03:09.726 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Subtract three quarters from one whole litre.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.495 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One quarter of a litre remains.

00:03:12.615 --> 00:03:17.371 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Check: three quarters poured out plus one quarter remaining makes one whole litre.

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:21.163 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You walk two thirds of a kilometre, then one sixth more.

00:03:21.283 --> 00:03:23.674 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
How far is that altogether?

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.815 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two thirds is four sixths.

00:03:30.935 --> 00:03:34.164 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Adding one sixth gives five sixths of a kilometre.

00:03:34.533 --> 00:03:38.733 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to solve and explain everyday fraction problems.

00:03:38.853 --> 00:03:43.636 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Identify the whole, model the relationship, calculate and check the unit.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.355 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Could you invent a fraction story about your own day?

00:03:47.475 --> 00:03:51.157 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Give it a try, then explore the practice on Math Magic.
